Regarding power factor, in the past when nonlinear loads were fewer and motor loads dominated, power factor typically stood at 0.8. Now, with the widespread adoption of new loads like LED lighting, load characteristics have significantly changed, leading to generally higher power factors—often reaching 0.9 without compensation. Power factor improvement can be achieved through power system analysis, where required reactive power compensation capacity is determined during load flow calculations.

Lightning surge overvoltage has already been discussed. Regarding harmonic mitigation, there are primarily the following methods:

First, using harmonic filters;
Second, employing harmonic mitigating transformers;
Third, striving to use three-phase balanced loads to reduce harmonic effects, though this method is relatively difficult to implement.

In comparison, the first two approaches are more feasible.
